SM(567,{"n":"2 Corinthians 7 Continued","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/2 Corinthians 7 Continued.mp3","d":2386.4,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.6,"Verse 5. For indeed, when we came to Macedonia, our bodies had no rest, but we were troubled on every side."],[30.0,"Outside were conflicts, inside were fears."],[33.52,"Nevertheless, God who comforts the downcast comforted us by the coming of Titus, and not only by his coming, but also by the consolation with which he was comforted in you, when he told us of your earnest desire, your mourning, your zeal for me, so that I rejoiced even more."],[49.6,"So now he's talking about how they've brought him joy, specifically."],[53.22,"So he says, We're having all these troubles in Macedonia."],[56.84,"Talk about the Roman province of Macedonia."],[60.24,"Saying we've been having all these hassles."],[62.21,"All these problems outside, on the outside there's trouble, and then on the inside, meaning on his, he's got his own worries and fears and doubts, about what's going on."],[72.71,"And in all these struggles, Saint Titus, who he's later going to write a letter to, came from Corinth and sort of gave him an update, on what was going on there after things had gone badly."],[85.07,"He got this update, and this update encouraged him, is what he's saying."],[89.23,"Encouraged him and brought him comfort because he talked about three things."],[93.51,"Their earnest desire, that's the desire to be reconciled."],[97.23,"The fact that you want to put things back together."],[100.13,"Which is a big thing."],[102.05,"I mean, this was basically a church split."],[102.37,"A lot of church splits afterwards, the people have no desire to get back together"],[110.01,"They're perfectly happy to go their separate ways."],[112.57,"So St. Paul's saying, I was greatly encouraged that you guys want, to try to piece this back together."],[121.73,"And secondly, you're mourning, meaning the fact that they they treated this split, these people who had been alienated from the church, that they were mourning that."],[132.05,"Again, it wasn't good riddance."],[134.29,"Another temptation. Because they had a lot of problems before."],[138.85,"Now a bunch of people have been kind of kicked out and left."],[142.61,"It'd be very easy for the people still there to go, well, good riddance."],[146.29,"Now we can get back to peace and harmony and doing our thing"],[152.47,"And the people who are on the outside could have just said, Well, forget that Christianity nonsense."],[158.31,"That was a, you know, I tried that out and that's bogus."],[161.37,"And gone. That isn't what happened."],[163.13,"That they were mourning what had happened."],[165.05,"They were sad about what had happened."],[166.89,"That's a good thing."],[168.57,"When people come to me for marriage counseling, one of the first questions I ask them is if they fight."],[174.89,"If they tell me yes, they fight all the time, I'm greatly encouraged."],[179.85,"Because that means they still care about each other."],[182.49,"Because if you don't care about somebody, they can't make you mad."],[185.93,"Or sad. Or upset."],[188.79,"When you read something off."],[189.91,"If I have people come to me for marriage counseling and they tell me they never fight anymore, I know it's in a bad place."],[200.03,"Paul is saying, you guys haven't shut down like that."],[203.97,"You still care."],[205.57,"You still care."],[206.53,"This is a wound to you, what's happened."],[209.97,"And so since you see that it's a wound, we can work on healing it."],[214.69,"So this is a good thing."],[217.17,"This is a good thing."],[218.53,"And then finally, their zeal for him."],[221.01,"They still care about him."],[223.25,"And they sent Titus saying, tell us what's going on with Paul."],[228.29,"Let him know we're thinking about, we're praying for him."],[230.61,"So say Paul's going, good, they haven't written me off either."],[233.33,"After things went so badly"],[236.33,"Again, it would have been very easy for them to say, Well, I hope that Paul guy never comes back."],[240.57,"His last visit was miserable."],[243.27,"But they haven't done that either."],[245.43,"Well, there's a difference between their situation and modern situation."],[251.43,"Because today, if there's a church split and there wind up being two churches, that's just two more among the many thousands of churches."],[259.75,"Yeah. At this time, there's just Christianity."],[264.17,"Yeah. You're not gonna be a Christian anymore."],[267.05,"Yeah. Yeah. It isn't in anybody's mind that it would be to an alternative Christian community."],[275.03,"Yeah. And but my point in all that is not just to point out that difference, but to point out that it's a bad thing for us."],[284.75,"Yeah. We think, oh, sure, we can have more churches."],[289.76,"Yeah. It's much more of a temptation for us."],[292.32,"Exactly. It's much easier for us to decide it's okay."],[295.92,"Yes. Well, they left our church, but they're going to that other church on the other side of town and they seem happy there, so fine."],[301.2,"It's much more of a temptation for us."],[303.44,"At least at least in the first century, you know, when St."],[307.76,"Paul's making these arguments, it's not you need to stop going to that guy's church and come back to the original church."],[313.52,"It's like you can't go back to worshiping pagan gods."],[316.57,"Like, there's a more clear-cut thing."],[318.67,"You're either in the Christian community or you're out there worshiping pagan gods."],[321.23,"There's kind of not a middle ground"],[324.71,"Yeah, so it's much more of a temptation for us to not see these kinds of issues in our church community as a wound that needs to be healed."],[334.95,"It's very easy for us to just see it as like well, we have a very consumer mentality towards everything, including religion now, anyway."],[344.31,"You know, where we go and it's sort of like we go to a church and it's like, oh, I kind of like it, and then after a while it's like, eh, I don't know, I'll go check out some other ones, see if there's what I like better."],[356.81,"And so we kind of shop around and you know, and do that."],[360.49,"And that's, you know, that's not the picture we're getting of what the church is."],[365.75,"Because you don't do that with your family."],[367.67,"Some of us might want to occasionally do that with our family."],[370.55,"But you don't."],[371.75,"You don't. If you and one of your siblings are, you know, not talking to each other."],[378.49,"You see that as a wound."],[380.17,"That's a problem."],[381.69,"We need to fix that."],[382.97,"Or if there's something between you and one of your parents, that's a wound."],[386.98,"That we live with."],[388.18,"So the same thing should apply."],[390.58,"It's not like, you know, when I was a kid I could get fed up with my parents."],[394.02,"I don't like you guys as my mom and dad anymore."],[395.94,"I'm gonna go move in with Billy, and his mom and dad will be my mom and dad."],[400.28,"It doesn't work that way."],[402.17,"Like, and so the the church, the church doesn't work that way either, really"],[406.17,"Really we can try and pretend it does. but the spiritual reality of it is that it doesn't"],[413.65,"We're saying, for even if I made you sorry with my letter, he's talking again about that letter he called the sorrowful letter."],[420.69,"I do not regret it, though I did regret it."],[423.97,"For I perceive that the same epistle made you sorry, though only for a while."],[428.45,"So he says, at first when I sent it to you, and I, you know, he he kind of regret like maybe I was a little harsh."],[437.39,"Maybe I was a little, you know, and we don't know because this is a is an epistle we don't have."],[442.16,"Maybe, you know, just that, but now St."],[445.68,"Paul looking back on it says, now I don't regret it."],[448.4,"Because I see that, yeah, it upset you."],[451.44,"Some of the things I said upset you. but only for a little while"],[455.37,"Meaning you got over the upset part, you got over the offended part, and now that's inspired you, to action."],[464.75,"And this is this is again, if you you talk about a parent and children, if if your parenting strategy is I never want to do anything to get my kids mad at me or upset them, first of all, you're gonna drive yourself insane, but second of all, you're gonna be a lousy parent."],[481.01,"Sometimes you have to tell them things or do things that are gonna upset them, but they're only gonna be upset for a little while, and they'll get over it."],[489.73,"And in the long run, it's what they need to hear or what you need to do."],[493.25,"And so that's what St."],[494.29,"Paul is saying here."],[495.17,"I said some things that I know upset you, but you know, and I worried a little, but now I'm glad I did because I see you were upset for a little while, but now they've sort of moved you in the right direction."],[507.45,"So do we read between the lines that these people who've been outside are in some sense, whether it's formal or not, sort of applying to come back yet."],[520.17,"Well, St. Paul seems concerned to make sure that they all are."],[525.29,"But yeah, the way he talks about it, at least some of them are."],[530.97,"Trying to find a strategy to re enter."],[533.21,"That's very hard to do."],[534.89,"I mean, again, today it's very easy."],[537.31,"You get kicked out of one church, or one church tries to discipline you in some way, you just go to the one down the street."],[543.37,"And say, Oh, well, they were a lousy church."],[546.53,"This could be a pretty serious thing, not just one family, like Fanalee."],[550.39,"But if you've been told, you're the Potter Familius of your household, and you've been told, look, until you shape up about XYZ, there's the door."],[560.39,"That's a blow."],[562.85,"To your pride."],[564.43,"To your and this is the Roman, this is Roman culture."],[568.51,"Honor and shame are big, are a big deal."],[572.29,"You've just been sort of publicly shamed."],[574.7,"About your business."],[575.84,"The one person we know about is the person in 1 Corinthians who was sleeping with his mother-in-law."],[580.16,"You've just had that business put out there that you were doing. that and been told to leave until you repent"],[586.68,"Coming back and fessing up and repenting, trying to make things right, that's hard at that point."],[595.24,"That's gonna require a lot of humility, a lot of love."],[599.18,"A lot of love for God."],[600.7,"That's what St."],[601.66,"Paul is trying to make sure happens."],[604.3,"And, you know, being called out publicly for that kind of thing will upset someone."],[609.6,"I mean, that's gonna."],[611.2,"But if it upsets them in a way that gets them to change, then it's good that they were upset."],[617.6,"Because St. Paul wasn't trying to upset them to really get them to leave and never come back."],[621.76,"That's not what he wanted."],[623.06,"The goal was I need to upset you because you're too comfortable in a bad situation."],[626.34,"So I need to upset the situation to get you to change"],[632.78,"So let's say let's take a situation with our personal."],[639.66,"What if it never got out and remained a secret and they continued to go to church?"],[644.78,"So I well, nobody would know, so I mean you couldn't deal with it."],[650.22,"But obviously somebody had found out because St."],[653.74,"wrote about it in personality."],[655.18,"Yes, yeah. In First Corinthians, when St."],[656.86,"Paul's talking about it, it had gotten out."],[658.94,"But and he not only talked to them, he talked to the rest of there as a community, like, what are you doing?"],[664.14,"Just letting this go on and not saying anything."],[666.54,"Yeah, because they were ignoring it."],[667.9,"Yeah, just ignoring it."],[669.82,"Like, what are you thinking?"],[671.18,"Don't you care about this person"],[674.31,"So like if something happens today, you if the church doesn't know about it, they can't really do anything."],[680.23,"Yeah, well, yeah, if you I mean obviously you can't do anything if you don't know."],[683.83,"Yeah. But if if I'm sleeping with my mother-in-law, she's dead, so I'm not sure."],[689.19,"There's not a danger of that happening."],[690.55,"If I was sleeping with my mother-in-law years ago, uh and the church finds out about it, I would expect that the priest and and other people in the church would be upset by that fact."],[703.91,"Yeah, and would not just ignore it."],[707.35,"That the priest would be at my house giving me a talking to."],[713.27,"But this does still happen in churches all the time."],[715.51,"Where there's sort of an open secret"],[717.63,"Oh yes. Uh huh."],[721.23,"You know this person is doing that, or this person is doing that, or this is how this person is living."],[726.67,"And we all just and we all just agree not to talk about it."],[729.79,"Uh you know, like nobody ever says it, but like everybody knows."],[735.07,"That's the kind of situation that St."],[738.11,"Paul was addressing."],[739.07,"He's like, if you love each other, you have to fix this."],[744.35,"Because this person is not on the way to the kingdom of God, living that way."],[749.71,"So if you care about him at all, you have to straighten him out."],[753.33,"Have you ever heard of the that best on?"],[757.01,"Like how a straight out straightened out a child."],[760.57,"Well, I can't tell people."],[762.49,"Oh, it is no, it it is it is very common in the Orthodox Church that people will commit certain sins and have to refrain from taking communion for some period of time."],[779.13,"Which means they're functionally excommunicated."],[782.41,"Now we want them to come back to church, keep coming to church."],[785.93,"They just don't receive the sacrament."],[788.41,"Because part of that is coming back to the church and praying and repenting."],[792.19,"And straightening things out."],[794.27,"So it is very common for certain sins, once they're committed, that there's at least a period of time where you're cut off from the Eucharist."],[804.03,"Less common, thankfully, are issues where you have someone who's doing something like that and is unrepentant."],[811.13,"And doesn't want to confess it or wants to justify it."],[813.61,"And in those cases, the people have to be like literally put out of the church until they repent."],[820.41,"Yeah, but so exactly like what St."],[822.41,"Paul did, that's what we have to do sometimes."],[827.31,"But the the goal of cutting someone off from communion for a time is giving them a time of repentance."],[835.95,"Or you're meeting with them and working with them."],[839.4,"And trying to heal."],[840.94,"Heal the issues."],[841.82,"I mean, probably the most common one of those is probably people getting divorced."],[846.54,"Because when two people get divorced, it's never 100% one person's fault."],[852.74,"It may be 90%, 10%, but it's never 100% one person's fault."],[858.98,"And it's not just about repenting of the sins that led up to it."],[864.42,"It's also about healing the damage that's been done in the whole process of everything that's happened."],[871.38,"And so there's that's probably the most common because unfortunately divorces become very common in our society."],[877.3,"So that ends up becoming the most common reason that there ends up being a period of of several months where you're meeting with a person, where we're then talking about things, trying to assess what are the things that need to change, what are the ways in which they contributed to what happened."],[892.66,"ed. That they need to repent of and be heal of"],[896.84,"Um, what's the damage that's been done to them that they need to be healed of."],[901.32,"So, like, but what if something like, let's say I'm married and my wife cheats on me."],[906.2,"Would that be any of my fault at all?"],[908.04,"Or would that be completely?"],[909.16,"It depends. I mean, it depends."],[911.8,"I'd have to talk to you."],[913.56,"Because sometimes one person goes and commits adultery because of the way they're being treated by the other person."],[920.2,"I mean, it's like I said, it's never 100% zero percent."],[923.82,"Sometimes it's 90-10."],[926.19,"But there's always because all of us are sinners."],[930.91,"I can't sit here and say I've never sinned against my wife in any way."],[935.64,"There have been times where I've been short with her, been rude to her, gotten angry with her."],[936.2,"And vice versa"],[941.42,"So I mean, that's going to be true in any marriage."],[944.94,"And so in a marriage that ends up breaking apart, that's going to be doubly true."],[949.64,"That there were some things."],[951.42,"And like I said, it may be lopsided in terms of, you know, but it's never, there are no perfect people."],[959.42,"Well, I think the example you give, if if your wife's been cheating on you and you discover that, that puts a tremendous burden on you."],[970.94,"What do I do now?"],[973.58,"And what is the right thing to do?"],[975.82,"Yeah. I'm not sure anyone, I'm not sure that anyone knows what the right thing to do in a circumstance like that is."],[982.98,"Because there's a thousand details we don't know in that hypothetical."],[986.66,"Yeah. And so a lot of times, unfortunately, we're faced with decisions that are decisions between lesser of evils."],[998.68,"You know, uh two wrong choices are the only choices I have."],[1004.04,"It's still a wrong choice if it was the lesser of the two."],[1008.52,"It's the right choice to make, but it's still a problem."],[1011.64,"So you still have to, you still have to confront that problem."],[1016.45,"Yeah. But enough about voting."],[1018.61,"Um. So he says, that he ultimately is not sorry about his his sorrowful letter because it upset them, but only for a time."],[1031.31,"Verse 9. Now I rejoice, not that you were made sorry, but that your sorrow led to repentance."],[1036.67,"So he says, I'm not happy now that I, you know, offended or upset you."],[1040.89,"He's that's not his goal."],[1042.35,"But he's saying, I'm happy that by doing that, that's led you now to repentance."],[1049.31,"There were some sins that were, like in the case of soldiers defending Constantinople from invasion, were asked to excommunicate themselves from the church for months, in case in some cases years."],[1062.67,"Yeah. Even in service to defense of Constantinople."],[1067.55,"Because yeah, having having to take a human life does damage to you"],[1068.15,"Yeah. They took it that seriously."],[1075.19,"And anybody who's ever done it knows that full well."],[1079.09,"And that needs to be healed."],[1081.46,"That needs to be healed."],[1085.22,"Since for you were made sorry in a godly manner that you might suffer loss from us in nothing."],[1092.28,"So he says, I upset you in a godly way."],[1097.9,"That's I didn't just I mean I didn't just upset you like by I mean, I could insult, yeah, I can insult someone and get them upset."],[1105.18,"I can troll someone on the internet and get them upset, that's he's saying I didn't just go and get you upset."],[1110.92,"But it was in a godly way"],[1113.5,"I had an intent behind it."],[1115.51,"Which was to get bring you to repentance and bring you back to God."],[1119.11,"That's why I upset you."],[1121.27,"Not just because I was mad at you, and I wanted to take a shot, or whatever, but with the with the goal in mind of bringing you to repentance."],[1130.75,"For godly sorrow produces repentance, leading to salvation, not to be regretted, but the sorrow of the world produces death."],[1141.23,"So he's contrasting two kinds of sorrow."],[1144.61,"Two kinds of getting upset, two kinds of sort of anger and frustration."],[1148.91,"So he says there's godly sorrow that brings you to repentance, and there's worldly sorrow that brings you to death."],[1153.71,"He's saying I wasn't trying to get you to fall into despair"],[1153.99,"What's worldly sorrow?"],[1155.11,"Worldly sorrow then is like despair."],[1160.95,"I wasn't trying to depress you."],[1162.55,"I wasn't trying to harm you."],[1163.99,"I wasn't trying to drive you away."],[1166.8,"Or put you off."],[1168.34,"I was trying to bring you to repentance."],[1170.9,"And this is something I end up talking about a lot with people in confession."],[1177.3,"Because the issue with sin in our lives and with the struggle with sin in our lives, we folk tend to focus on how often we fall, how often we mess up."],[1189.32,"How often we do the wrong thing and get off track."],[1192.38,"And then how often we have to come to confession with the same things a lot of the time, over and over again."],[1196.38,"That's what we focus on"],[1199.78,"But what God focuses on is how many times we get back up and get back on track."],[1207.4,"And go back to following Him."],[1209.72,"It's that second part that's important."],[1211.88,"And that's the distinction St."],[1213.64,"Paul is making here."],[1215.0,"The devil doesn't have to work very hard to get me to sin."],[1218.68,"In fact, he probably doesn't have to do much."],[1221.49,"I'm pretty good at just sinning on my own through my own weakness and stupidity and desire for just wicked things."],[1228.85,"But where the devil comes in is trying to make me stay in the ditch I fall into, telling me that that ditch is where I belong, and I should just stay there and wallow in the mud."],[1239.57,"That's that's where he comes in."],[1240.69,"Feeling sorry for myself, hating myself, thinking I'm no good"],[1241.45,"And that's this worldly sorrow."],[1248.25,"And that just leads to death, like St."],[1250.97,"Paul says. So if the prodigal son said, well, I live."],[1255.69,"Yeah, if the prodigal son just stayed with the pigs and died."],[1258.49,"Yeah. That's that's the worldly sorrow he's talking about."],[1262.41,"The godly sorrow he's talking about is sorrow, where I look at myself and realize what I've done and mourn for it, and I'm upset about it, sad about it, but rather than giving in to despair, that drives me to come back."],[1277.09,"And to strive to do better and to look to be healed."],[1281.91,"To draw closer to Christ rather than giving up."],[1284.31,"So that's what he's talking about here"],[1286.67,"He's saying, I'm glad my letter upset you because it caused this godly sorrow that's led you to repentance."],[1294.75,"And he didn't want to cause the worldly sorrow."],[1297.73,"He didn't want them to fall into despair and think they were unworthy and be ashamed, you know, and never come back."],[1306.37,"For observe this very thing that you sorrowed in a godly manner, what diligence it produced in you, what clearing."],[1315.57,"Notice those are in italics, so I'm going to leave it out."],[1318.29,"What clearing, ign indignation, fear, vehement desire, zeal, and vindication."],[1325.25,"In all things, you proved yourselves to be clear in this matter."],[1328.61,"So he says, look at what the result was"],[1331.61,"He says, I know what I said upset you, but look at what the result of that has been."],[1336.9,"The result of that has been look at the diligence that produced in you, the discipline."],[1343.3,"When you discipline someone, the goal is to instill discipline in them."],[1348.2,"Self-discipline. He says, and uh what clearing, what clearing, meaning what clarity."],[1355.02,"What clarity that gave you about your own life and about what was going on."],[1359.58,"What indignation. Not indignation at St."],[1364.46,"Paul, like, how dare you speak to me that way, but indignation at themselves."],[1369.42,"Looking at going, I'm better than this."],[1372.06,"I can do better than this."],[1373.46,"I can be better than."],[1374.34,"I'm angry at myself that I did that."],[1377.06,"That I messed up and I did that."],[1379.38,"What fear. Again, that fear being awe and reverence."],[1382.74,"And that's part of what develops that is the awareness of our own sinfulness and our own failings, is what helps us develop that awe and reverence toward God."],[1391.48,"And who He is."],[1393.56,"What vehement desire."],[1395.5,"I like vehement, the New King James translation there."],[1399.5,"But the idea that now they have this desire to come back."],[1403.35,"This desire to come back."],[1405.11,"They're missing being a part of the community, which they may have taken for granted before."],[1409.99,"They're missing the Eucharist, which they may have been taking for granted before."],[1415.59,"They're missing the fellowship that they may have taken for granted before."],[1419.39,"What zeal, same idea."],[1422.13,"They're now zealous."],[1423.41,"They want to go back, you know, to the community, which they may not have wanted to before St."],[1429.09,"Paul sent them that letter and upset them."],[1433.49,"And what vindication."],[1435.19,"The vindication being, you know, at the end of the day."],[1439.77,"When you were sitting there wallowing in the worldly sorrow of I'm no good."],[1444.11,"I'm worthless, etc."],[1446.27,"etc. But now there's this vindication that no, that's not who you are."],[1452.04,"That's not who you are in Christ."],[1453.88,"Verse 12 Therefore, although I wrote to you, I did not do it for for the sak thee sak ofe him of who him who had done the wrong, nor suffered wrong, but that our care for you in the sight of God might appear to you."],[1467.68,"So he's saying, I wasn't taking sides."],[1471.52,"I wasn't writing to you on behalf of the people who were sinned against, saying, Hey, you need to repent of what you did to them."],[1479.76,"And vice versa."],[1481.22,"He wasn't writing on behalf of the people who had sinned to the people they sinned against, saying, Hey, you need to forgive those people."],[1487.86,"He's saying he was writing on behalf of, ultimately, on behalf of uh that his his care for them in the sight of God might appear to you."],[1496.84,"He's writing on behalf of God to them."],[1499.72,"And his desire to see them all reconciled to God."],[1502.6,"He says, That's where I'm coming from."],[1503.56,"ing I'm not pick a side."],[1503.64,"I want all of you to be reconciled to each other and reconciled to God"],[1510.48,"That's the perspective I'm coming from."],[1512.64,"Therefore, we have been comforted in your comfort, and we rejoiced exceedingly more for the joy of Titus, because his spirit has been refreshed by you all."],[1522.4,"For if in anything I have boasted to him about you, I am not ashamed."],[1526.4,"But as we spoke all things to you in truth, even so our boasting Titus was found true, I had told he came to you."],[1539.36,"And St. Paul is saying, and when he went there and he met you, and encountered you."],[1544.68,"St. Paul's saying, I wasn't embarrassed about what I had told him, because you didn't prove me wrong."],[1548.12,"You didn't prove me wrong"],[1551.52,"And so when he brought back this good report, that made me happy."],[1556.25,"That made me happy too."],[1557.93,"That not because, oh hey, everything's perfect now."],[1560.57,"Otherwise, he wouldn't be writing this letter."],[1562.67,"Or he'd be writing a very different letter."],[1564.99,"It'd be much shorter."],[1567.95,"Glad everything's going well."],[1569.71,"You know, see you in a couple of years."],[1571.47,"Say hi to these people."],[1572.91,"You know, that's but that things are on the right track."],[1578.11,"Things are where they need to be."],[1579.95,"The attitude, the desires of the people is for this reconciliation to happen."],[1584.91,"And since everyone's desiring that, that means it can happen."],[1588.83,"And his affections are greater for you as he remembers the obedience of you all, how with fear and trembling you received him."],[1595.51,"Therefore, I rejoice that I have confidence in you in everything."],[1600.07,"So he says, uh, and he now, Titus, now that he's met you, he loves you too."],[1607.28,"And so, um, and so uh he remembers how you received him."],[1613.76,"That again, the fear and trembling there is talking about that that respect."],[1618.41,"That you received him with respect because you knew he was coming from they didn't know him before, but they received him with this respect and decency and this good reception because of he was coming from Saint Paul."],[1630.09,"And so St."],[1631.61,"Paul can say, Therefore, I rejoice that I have confidence in you in everything."],[1634.57,"confidence in. Not meaning you're doing everything right and everything's perfect, but he has in them everything"],[1641.89,"He believes that they can do what he's asking them to do."],[1647.49,"That they can make this happen."],[1649.73,"Yes, sir. Do you ever read this chapter in the lectionary?"],[1652.93,"Because it seems so personal."],[1655.73,"It's much more personal than it is theological."],[1660.45,"Yeah. This seems more like a letter to people."],[1663.89,"Yeah, it it is in the lectionary."],[1666.61,"It is on weekdays, though."],[1668.45,"Uh-huh. It's not in the regular Sunday lectionary."],[1673.25,"And that's probably because, as you say, it's very personal."],[1677.25,"You would have to have some idea of the context for it to have value as teaching."],[1686.57,"Yeah. And so with the with the lectionary selections that are on weekdays, you're kind of going through lectio continua."],[1693.69,"You're right. You're going through it's a set of verses on Monday, the next verses on Tuesday, the next verses on Wednesday."],[1701.29,"Whereas you've noticed, you especially since you read the epistle, have noticed that the Sunday epistle readings jump around a lot."],[1709.13,"And tend to be more sort of theologically oriented passages."],[1712.97,"Where the weekday readings just sort of go through all of the books of the New Testament."],[1722.11,"So if you yeah, if you follow those, read readings dailyings, every year you'll read through the whole New Testament except for the book of Revelation, which isn't in the lectionary."],[1733.67,"But yeah, so this is one of the sections that's on the that's on the weekdays."],[1740.15,"Yeah. And part of the the point you're making is important because uh as we'll see as we're going through St."],[1747.83,"Paul's letters, a lot of St."],[1749.99,"Paul's letters are very personal."],[1751.83,"This is one of the first of all, we haven't often translated them that way."],[1757.03,"There's sort of a and it it developed."],[1759.75,"I mean, the the King James Version in 1611 was sort of normal English for 1611."],[1765.81,"Relatively at least."],[1767.41,"But the King James Version at some point between them then and the 19th century when the Oxford and Cambridge editions came out developed into sort of this Bible speak."],[1778.67,"Like its own form of biblical English."],[1782.91,"Maybe on Wednesday night called it ecclesiastical English."],[1785.95,"Yeah. Yeah, ecclesiastical English or liturgical English or something."],[1790.75,"Yeah. And that it's it's good when you're doing the public reading of scripture, like in the liturgy, to have sort of elevated language."],[1802.43,"So I'm not disparaging that at all."],[1805.39,"But that has caused us to not be in tune with how personal and occasional. the epistles are"],[1816.53,"I mean, the gospels and even Acts are written in a different sort of way."],[1821.6,"They're intended for this broad audience and that."],[1824.24,"But the epistles, even the epistles that aren't named after destinations."],[1828.96,"I mean, first and second Peter, first, second, third John."],[1831.44,"First, second, third John's a perfect example."],[1833.6,"They're these intensely personal letters written to communities that have this personal relationship with the person writing them."],[1841.74,"About issues that are going on right then, right there."],[1846.56,"So when in in 2 John, when St."],[1849.04,"John is talking about not receiving traveling teachers who are preaching another gospel, that's not just this sort of, well, here's here's how you as a Christian community should deal with heretics."],[1862.06,"Like on some, you know, practical theology kind of discurses."],[1867.42,"That's there were these guys showing up at the church that St."],[1872.7,"John is writing to, who were teaching them false things and they needed to not receive them."],[1879.39,"This was a direct thing."],[1882.67,"And I think that the reason I brought the translation issue in terms of us not reading them that way is that I don't think that it's uh a coincidence that a lot of reformed Protestant theology, especially, that came out of circumstances where people were only hearing the scriptures read corporately in elevated language, have, for example, sort of turned Romans into systematic theology instead of a letter from St."],[1921.03,"Paul to actual humans in the church in Rome, in the middle of the first century AD to deal with their circumstances."],[1931.81,"That kind of language and only hearing it in that context, that elevated context, shifts how you read it and understand it."],[1940.17,"And makes it much easier to sort of turn what you're reading into a bunch of theological abstractions. and disconnect it from life"],[1949.91,"And not just life in general, just life in terms of like morals or ethics or praxis, but I mean liturgy, the life of the community."],[1962.33,"Baptizing their children, having weddings, and and marriages and funerals and and all of these things, which is the circumstance in which these letters are produced, and which ground it."],[1976.46,"And you get to theological principles from that, but you're sort of, it becomes very easy to skip a step."],[1984.78,"Right It's almost like you're taking the flesh and blood out of it."],[1988.3,"Right. And so you come to Romans 8, and instead of Romans 8 being Saint Paul encouraging these people who are going through this time of struggle in their community to say, God has God has started this work of your salvation."],[2005.54,"He started it before you were born, some of you."],[2009.63,"And Christ died for you while you were still out there, a bunch of heathen sinners."],[2015.07,"God's been doing all that."],[2016.19,"And now he's started this work in you."],[2018.43,"He's going to finish it."],[2020.27,"And instead of St."],[2021.31,"Paul talking about that, now we say, well, okay, here's the order of salvation."],[2026.35,"There's justification, and then there's sanctification, and then there's glorification, and they happen in this order, and this one happens at one time, and this one is a process, and this one starts completely foreign to anything St."],[2038.63,"Paul is talking about."],[2041.03,"And suddenly no one would put in 11."],[2043.45,"And then we get to the next chapter, and St."],[2047.05,"Paul is talking about an objection to that."],[2050.65,"Because someone comes back to him and says, Okay, you're saying God started this with us and he's going to bring it to conclusion, but didn't he start it with Israel?"],[2058.73,"And that all went haywire."],[2060.57,"And so in the next chapter, St."],[2062.09,"Paul has to talk about, in chapters 9 through 11, no, it didn't go haywire."],[2067.21,"This has been part of God's plan all along."],[2069.29,"And explain. Going through the Old Testament."],[2072.41,"Here's Israel, and here is how ultimately at the end of chapter 11, all Israel is going to be saved, and God will have kept his promise."],[2077.13,"All of his promises that he began, he's going to bring it to conclusion"],[2083.81,"And we turn that into God picked some people to go to heaven and some people to go to hell before he created the world."],[2092.05,"And the nation of Israel is going to convert to Christianity in the end times."],[2097.33,"And all of this."],[2107.37,"900 years later was going to convert to Christianity in the end times."],[2112.33,"Like what relevance does God theoretically picking people before he created the world to be saved or go to hell have to do with anything he's talking to them about or anything they're worried about."],[2126.09,"But we're not reading it as this personal letter from St."],[2128.97,"Paul to other people."],[2130.17,"We're reading it in this elevated abstract this is this church book."],[2135.93,"This is God telling us theological principles."],[2139.93,"How do they fit together?"],[2142.17,"How do we apply them?"],[2145.21,"This would take care of the very common objection that I've heard a thousand times that Christ had a religion, and then Paul came along and created a religion about Christ."],[2160.01,"Yeah. No, Paul is well the Muslims, no doubt."],[2163.77,"Yeah, yeah. Paul is not doing that in these."],[2166.41,"Nowhere in the New Testament is anyone creating a religion."],[2167.13,"Yes Exactly. No one is."],[2172.77,"The concept of a religion didn't exist until the 17th century."],[2176.85,"Nor did the category of monotheism, but that's a whole other topic."],[2182.21,"But um, but yeah, there was no concept of a religion."],[2186.21,"Yeah, there's all kinds of weird abstractions we do."],[2190.05,"You know, people talk about Saint Paul converting."],[2193.65,"Like there was this religion Judaism and this religion Christianity, and he left one and joined the other in 35 AD."],[2203.23,"Like, it's not even possible that that's what happened."],[2206.03,"Uh-huh. Yeah. So all of these later theological categories, we go and shoehorn back into the Bible."],[2212.15,"Yeah, and I and I think part of that is that elevated reading, where we've abstracted the scriptures themselves from the life of the church, the historical life of the church, in which they are firmly embedded in the historical life of the church."],[2239.43,"Same for me."],[2240.23,"Yeah. Church discipline, the whole nine yards."],[2246.73,"St. Paul is this rabbi teaching a systematic theology class."],[2250.95,"In that view."],[2252.13,"And that's not at all who he is."],[2253.97,"Yeah, I I had the impression."],[2255.61,"He's an apostle and a pastor and a spiritual father."],[2259.13,"Yes, that's who he is."],[2260.81,"And an evangelist."],[2262.97,"I got the feeling that he wrote theology with a introduction to people and a sign off at the end."],[2270.49,"Yeah. That it was pure theology in between."],[2273.67,"That's what I always thought."],[2276.39,"Yeah. Um. Yeah."],[2278.57,"And that's, you know, sort of stripping away Saint Paul's humanity on top of everything else."],[2283.85,"He's just this talking head."],[2285.29,"That's why it doesn't seem very appealing to people, is that we've stripped away the human side of it."],[2291.93,"Yeah. Yeah. We don't see him as a person, we see him as proclamations."],[2298.02,"Right And that's one of the things I think that's actually helpful in the Orthodox Church about iconography, that we have these visible representations of the human person."],[2307.19,"Like in my office, I've got an icon of the Prophet Moses, a big icon of Saint Paul, and one of St."],[2314.47,"Jerome. Those are sort of my Bible icons."],[2317.68,"But when you're when you're reading Saint Paul and thinking about St."],[2321.04,"Paul, having an icon of Saint Paul there, which is depicting pretty much what he looked like."],[2326.29,"Balding. You know, kind of a pointy beard, short, hunched over."],[2331.87,"That's that's the human whose letter you're reading."],[2335.47,"And even even the I gotta go to the prophet Moses."],[2338.43,"Like it's not Charlton Heston."],[2342.09,"You know. He's this guy trying to lead these people who keep repelling against him and wanting to kill him."],[2348.25,"But he still loves them and is trying to lead them and trying to get them to where they need to go."],[2352.89,"You know. So he's this human."],[2354.86,"So let me go ahead, we'll we'll end here and then we can So thank you everybody."],[2359.26,"We'll pick up uh with chapter eight."],[2361.42,"Next time. Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2371.34,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancient faith.com."],[2376.7,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.6,"lv":2,"h":"2 Corinthians 7:5-7 - Comfort through Titus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:5-7</b>: in <b>Macedonia</b> \"our bodies had no rest... <b>outside were conflicts, inside were fears</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"God who <b>comforts the downcast</b> comforted us by the coming of <b>Titus</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Titus also comforted by <b>them</b> - reported their <b>earnest desire, mourning, zeal for me</b> → \"I rejoiced even more\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Now talking about how they've <b>brought him joy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Macedonia = the <b>Roman province</b>; trouble <b>outside</b>, and <b>inside</b> his own worries, fears, doubts</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Titus</b> (Paul later writes him a letter) came from Corinth with an <b>update after things went badly</b> → encouraged him</li></ul>"},{"t":89.23,"lv":3,"h":"Their desire, mourning and zeal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Three things in Titus's report</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1. Earnest desire</b> = to be <b>reconciled</b>, put things back together</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a big thing\" - it was basically a <b>church split</b></li><li class=\"d1\">many splits afterwards: <b>no desire to get back together</b>, happy to go separate ways</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2. Mourning</b> - the split wasn't <b>\"good riddance\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">temptation for those left: back to peace and harmony</li><li class=\"d1\">temptation for those outside: \"forget that Christianity nonsense\"</li><li class=\"d1\">neither happened - <b>sad about it</b> = good thing</li><li class=\"d0\">Marriage counseling: first question, <b>\"do you fight?\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">yes, all the time → <b>encouraged</b> - they still care; \"if you don't care, they can't make you mad or sad\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we never fight anymore\" → <b>\"in a bad place\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: you haven't <b>shut down</b> - a <b>wound</b> we can work on healing</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3. Zeal for him</b> - they sent Titus to say <b>we're praying for you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"they haven't <b>written me off</b> either\" - easy to say \"hope Paul never comes back, last visit was miserable\"</li></ul>"},{"t":245.43,"lv":2,"h":"Church splits then and now","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Difference from today: a split now = <b>two more among thousands</b> of churches</li><li class=\"d1\">then: <b>just Christianity</b> - no alternative Christian community</li><li class=\"d1\">leaving = going back to <b>pagan gods</b>; no middle ground</li><li class=\"d0\">But his point: it's <b>a bad thing for us</b>, much more <b>temptation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"they left our church, but they're at the <b>other church across town</b> and seem happy\" - fine</li><li class=\"d0\">We don't see these issues as <b>a wound to be healed</b></li></ul>"},{"t":334.95,"lv":3,"h":"Consumer mentality vs. family","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Consumer mentality</b> toward everything, including religion</li><li class=\"d1\">try a church, \"eh,\" <b>shop around</b> for what I like better</li><li class=\"d1\">not the picture of the church</li><li class=\"d0\">You don't do that with <b>family</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not talking to a sibling = <b>a wound, a problem, we need to fix it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">something between you and a parent = a wound we live with</li><li class=\"d1\">as a kid: \"I'm gonna <b>move in with Billy</b>, his mom and dad will be my mom and dad\" - <b>doesn't work that way</b></li><li class=\"d0\">The church doesn't either - \"we can pretend it does, but the <b>spiritual reality</b> is that it doesn't\"</li></ul>"},{"t":413.65,"lv":2,"h":"7:8 - The sorrowful letter, no regret","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:8</b>: \"even if I made you sorry with my letter, <b>I do not regret it</b>, though I did regret it... that epistle made you sorry, though only <b>for a while</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The <b>sorrowful letter</b> - an epistle <b>we don't have</b></li><li class=\"d1\">at first regretted it - \"maybe I was a little harsh\"</li><li class=\"d1\">now: <b>no regret</b> - it upset you but only a little while</li><li class=\"d1\">you got over the <b>offended part</b>, and it <b>inspired you to action</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Parenting: \"never upset my kids\" strategy</li><li class=\"d1\">you'll <b>drive yourself insane</b> and be a <b>lousy parent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes must say or do things that upset them - <b>they get over it</b>; long run it's what they need</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: \"I worried a little, but now I'm glad\" - it <b>moved you in the right direction</b></li></ul>"},{"t":507.45,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: re-entering after being put out","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Are those outside in some sense <b>applying to come back</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: Paul seems concerned to make sure they <b>all</b> are; at least <b>some</b> are, <b>trying to find a strategy to re-enter</b> - very hard</li><li class=\"d1\">today: kicked out or disciplined → <b>go to the church down the street</b>, \"they were a lousy church\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Then: a <b>pater familias</b> told \"until you shape up, there's the door\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>blow to your pride</b> - Roman culture: <b>honor and shame</b> big</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>publicly shamed</b> about your business</li><li class=\"d1\">the one person we know: the man in <b>1 Cor</b> <b>sleeping with his mother-in-law</b> - business put out there, told to leave until repent</li><li class=\"d0\">Coming back, fessing up, making things right: <b>humility, love, love for God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Upsetting someone is good if it gets them to <b>change</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul didn't want them to leave for good: \"<b>too comfortable in a bad situation</b>, need to upset the situation to get you to change\"</li></ul>"},{"t":632.78,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: secrets and open secrets","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: What if it had <b>stayed a secret</b> and they kept going to church?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: If nobody knows, <b>can't deal with it</b> - but in Corinth it <b>had gotten out</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul rebuked the <b>whole community</b>: \"what are you doing, just <b>letting this go on</b>? Don't you care about this person?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Today: if the church finds out, the priest and others would be <b>upset</b>, not ignore it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"the priest would be at my house <b>giving me a talking to</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Still happens in churches all the time: the <b>open secret</b></li><li class=\"d1\">everyone knows how this person is living, <b>agree not to talk about it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: \"if you love each other, <b>fix this</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">that person is <b>not on the way to the kingdom of God</b> living that way</li><li class=\"d1\">\"if you care about him at all, you have to <b>straighten him out</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":753.33,"lv":3,"h":"Withholding communion as discipline","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Very common in the Orthodox Church</b>: certain sins → <b>refrain from Communion for a period</b></li><li class=\"d1\">functionally <b>excommunicated</b>, but <b>keep coming to church</b> - just don't receive the sacrament</li><li class=\"d1\">time for <b>praying, repenting, straightening things out</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Less common: <b>unrepentant</b>, won't confess or wants to <b>justify</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">then <b>literally put out of the church</b> until they repent - \"exactly like what Paul did\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Goal of cutting off for a time = <b>repentance</b>; meeting, working with them, <b>healing the issues</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Most common case: <b>divorce</b></li><li class=\"d1\">never <b>100% one person's fault</b> - may be 90/10</li><li class=\"d1\">not only repenting of what led up to it - <b>healing the damage</b> of the whole process</li><li class=\"d1\">several months meeting: what needs to change, how they <b>contributed</b>, what <b>damage</b> they need healed from</li></ul>"},{"t":901.32,"lv":3,"h":"Q: a cheating spouse, and lesser evils","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: If my wife cheats, is any of it <b>my fault</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: <b>\"It depends\"</b> - sometimes adultery is because of <b>how they're treated</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"never 100%, never zero\" - <b>all of us are sinners</b></li><li class=\"d1\">himself: been short, rude, angry with his wife - and <b>vice versa</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a marriage that breaks apart: <b>doubly true</b>; \"there are <b>no perfect people</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Another voice: finding out puts a <b>tremendous burden</b> - what's the right thing?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not sure anyone knows\" - <b>thousand details</b> in a hypothetical</li><li class=\"d1\">often <b>lesser of evils</b> - two wrong choices; the lesser is still right to choose, but <b>still a problem to confront</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"But enough about <b>voting</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1018.61,"lv":2,"h":"7:9 - Sorrow that leads to repentance","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">He's <b>not sorry about the letter</b> - it upset them only <b>for a time</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:9</b>: \"Now I rejoice, not that you were made sorry, but that your <b>sorrow led to repentance</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not happy he upset them - <b>happy it led to repentance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: <b>soldiers defending Constantinople</b> asked to <b>excommunicate themselves</b> for months, sometimes <b>years</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even in service of the city's defense - <b>taking a human life does damage</b></li><li class=\"d1\">took it that seriously; anyone who's done it knows - <b>needs to be healed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Made sorry in a <b>godly manner</b>, that you might suffer loss from us in nothing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not just upsetting them - \"I can <b>insult someone</b>, <b>troll</b> someone on the internet\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>intent</b>: bring you to <b>repentance and back to God</b>, not \"I was mad and wanted to take a shot\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1130.75,"lv":2,"h":"7:10 - Godly sorrow vs. worldly sorrow","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:10</b>: \"<b>godly sorrow</b> produces repentance leading to salvation, not to be regretted; but the <b>sorrow of the world produces death</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Two kinds of upset/anger/frustration</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Worldly sorrow</b> = <b>despair</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I wasn't trying to depress you, harm you, <b>drive you away</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: What's worldly sorrow? → despair, as above</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Prodigal son</b></li><li class=\"d1\">stays with the pigs and <b>dies</b> = worldly sorrow</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>godly sorrow</b>: see what I've done, <b>mourn</b> it, but <b>don't give in to despair</b> → come back, strive, <b>look to be healed</b>, draw closer to Christ</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul glad the letter caused godly sorrow; <b>didn't want them ashamed</b>, thinking unworthy, never coming back</li></ul>"},{"t":1170.9,"lv":3,"h":"Falling vs. getting back up","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Heard in <b>confession</b> a lot</li><li class=\"d0\">We focus on <b>how often we fall</b>, come to confession with the same things over and over</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>God focuses on how many times we get back up</b> and follow him again - \"the second part is what's important\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The devil</li><li class=\"d1\">\"doesn't have to work very hard to get me to sin\" - good at it on my own: <b>weakness, stupidity, wicked desires</b></li><li class=\"d1\">his work: make me <b>stay in the ditch</b>, \"that's where I belong, wallow in the mud\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>feeling sorry for myself, hating myself, thinking I'm no good</b> = worldly sorrow → <b>death</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1306.37,"lv":2,"h":"7:11 - What diligence it produced","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:11</b>: \"what <b>diligence</b>, what <b>clearing</b>, <b>indignation</b>, <b>fear</b>, <b>vehement desire</b>, <b>zeal</b>, <b>vindication</b>... you proved yourselves <b>clear in this matter</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"clearing\" is in italics in the translation - leaves it out</li><li class=\"d0\">Look at the <b>result</b> of the upset</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Diligence</b> = <b>discipline</b>; goal of discipline is <b>self-discipline</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Clearing</b> = <b>clarity</b> about your life and what was going on</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Indignation</b> - not at Paul (\"how dare you\") but <b>at themselves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm better than this... angry at myself that I messed up\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Fear</b> = awe and reverence - <b>awareness of our sinfulness</b> develops it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Vehement desire</b> (likes NKJV) = desire to <b>come back</b></li><li class=\"d1\">missing the <b>community, Eucharist, fellowship</b> once taken for granted</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Zeal</b> - same idea, now want to go back</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Vindication</b> - not the <b>\"I'm worthless\"</b> of worldly sorrow: <b>\"that's not who you are in Christ\"</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1453.88,"lv":2,"h":"7:12-13a - Not taking sides","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:12</b>: \"I did not do it for the sake of him who had done the wrong, nor of him who suffered wrong, but that our <b>care for you in the sight of God</b> might appear to you\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not taking sides</li><li class=\"d1\">not writing for the wronged: \"you need to repent\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not writing for the sinners: \"you need to forgive those people\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Writing <b>on behalf of God</b>, his care for them, <b>desire to see all reconciled to each other and to God</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:13a</b>: \"we have been comforted in your comfort\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1512.64,"lv":2,"h":"7:13-16 - Titus and confidence","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:13-16</b>: rejoiced more for the <b>joy of Titus</b>, spirit <b>refreshed by you all</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul had <b>boasted to Titus</b> about them - \"not ashamed\"; <b>boasting found true</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they <b>didn't prove him wrong</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Report made him happy - <b>not because everything's perfect</b></li><li class=\"d1\">else he'd write \"a very different letter... much shorter: glad all's well, see you in a couple years\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but things are <b>on the right track</b>: their <b>desire is for reconciliation</b> → means it <b>can happen</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Titus's <b>affections greater</b> as he remembers their <b>obedience, with fear and trembling</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>respect</b>; they didn't know Titus but received him with decency because he came from <b>Paul</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"I rejoice that I have <b>confidence in you in everything</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"you're doing everything right\" - he believes they <b>can do what he's asking</b> and make it happen</li></ul>"},{"t":1649.73,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: is this chapter in the lectionary?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Do you read this chapter in the lectionary? It's so <b>personal</b>, more than theological</li><li class=\"d0\">A: Yes, but on <b>weekdays</b>, not Sundays</li><li class=\"d1\">probably because it's personal - need <b>context</b> for it to have value as teaching</li><li class=\"d0\">Weekday readings = <b><i>lectio continua</i></b>: next verses each day</li><li class=\"d1\">Sunday epistles <b>jump around</b>, more <b>theologically oriented</b></li><li class=\"d1\">follow weekday readings → <b>whole NT each year except Revelation</b> (not in the lectionary)</li></ul>"},{"t":1740.15,"lv":3,"h":"Epistles are personal; \"Bible-speak\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Many of Paul's letters are <b>very personal</b> - and we haven't translated them that way</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>KJV 1611</b> = normal English for 1611</li><li class=\"d1\">by the <b>19th century</b> (Oxford and Cambridge editions) became <b>\"Bible speak\"</b> - \"<b>ecclesiastical</b> / liturgical English\"</li><li class=\"d1\">good for <b>public reading</b> in liturgy - not disparaging</li><li class=\"d1\">but we've lost how <b>personal and occasional</b> the epistles are</li><li class=\"d0\">Gospels and Acts: <b>broad audience</b>; epistles are different</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>1, 2, 3 John</b>: \"intensely personal letters\" to communities with a <b>personal relationship</b>, about <b>issues right then</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>2 John</b>: not receiving traveling teachers = <b>these guys showing up at that church</b>, not abstract \"how to deal with heretics\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1882.67,"lv":2,"h":"Romans as systematic theology","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reformed Protestant theology: heard scripture only in <b>elevated language</b> → turned <b>Romans</b> into <b>systematic theology</b></li><li class=\"d1\">instead of a <b>letter from Paul to actual humans in Rome, mid-1st century</b></li><li class=\"d1\">elevated context → <b>theological abstractions</b>, disconnected from <b>life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Life = <b>liturgy, the life of the community</b>: <b>baptizing children, weddings, funerals</b></li><li class=\"d1\">you reach principles from that, but \"easy to skip a step\" - <b>flesh and blood taken out</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans 8</b>: Paul encouraging people in struggle</li><li class=\"d1\">God <b>started the work</b> of your salvation - before you were born, <b>Christ died for you while sinners</b> → <b>he'll finish it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">instead: <b>order of salvation</b> - justification, sanctification, glorification</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans 9-11</b>: objection - didn't God start with <b>Israel</b>? Did that go haywire?</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: <b>no</b>, part of God's plan; through the OT; <b>all Israel saved</b> at end of ch. 11 → <b>God keeps his promise</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we turn it into <b>predestination to heaven/hell</b> before creation, and Israel <b>converting in the end times</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"what relevance... to anything they're worried about?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not reading it as a <b>personal letter</b> but as <b>\"God telling us theological principles\"</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2145.21,"lv":3,"h":"\"Paul created a religion about Christ\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Common objection: <b>Christ had a religion</b>, Paul created one <b>about Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">reading as personal letters addresses it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>nowhere in the NT is anyone creating a religion</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Concept of a religion</b> didn't exist until the <b>17th century</b>; nor did <b>monotheism</b> as a category (\"whole other topic\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul \"<b>converting</b>\" from Judaism to Christianity in <b>35 AD</b> - \"not even possible\"</li><li class=\"d1\">later theological categories <b>shoehorned back into the Bible</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Part of it: scriptures <b>abstracted from the historical life of the church</b>, where they're embedded</li></ul>"},{"t":2239.43,"lv":2,"h":"Paul as a person, and icons","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul as a <b>rabbi teaching systematic theology class</b> - \"not at all who he is\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he's an <b>apostle, pastor, spiritual father, evangelist</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Impression shared: intro, <b>pure theology</b>, sign-off</li><li class=\"d1\">strips away Paul's <b>humanity</b> - \"just a talking head\", proclamations not a person</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Iconography helps</b>: visible representations of the <b>human person</b></li><li class=\"d1\">office: icons of <b>Moses, Paul, Jerome</b> (his \"Bible icons\")</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's icon shows what he looked like: <b>balding, pointy beard, short, hunched over</b> - \"the human whose letter you're reading\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Moses: \"not <b>Charlton Heston</b>\" - leading people who <b>rebel and want to kill him</b>, but still loves them and leads them</li></ul>"}],"next":"Next time: <b>2 Corinthians 8</b>"});
